\section{Identify Duplicates Before Following Live References}

Levine describes an approach to C++ templates in which each compiler invocation emits the copies it needs and the linker keeps just one. His examples include GNU \texttt{.gnu.linkonce.*} names and historical Windows COMDAT selection policies. Current ELF instead provides a standardized way to describe groups of sections, including groups flagged \verb|GRP_COMDAT|: duplicates are recognized by the same group signature, not merely by the same section name. At most one such group survives duplicate selection, and selecting a copy means selecting its related members together \cite[chapter~4]{levine2000linkers}. The \href{https://gabi.xinuos.com/elf/03-sheader.html#section-groups}{ELF generic ABI section group rules} specify how discarded definitions and references are represented.

A reference from outside a group to a global or weak symbol in the group can resolve to the definition in a surviving copy. A local identity defined in a discarded group cannot be rebound to a same-spelled local identity from another file; ordinary external-to-group references to such locals are disallowed. Two ordinary strong definitions, with no duplicate-selection contract, remain an error. A weak-definition tie is not group selection: choosing a weak definition does not itself discard its section or associated data. Nor does ELF COMDAT selection promise to check that duplicate groups have equal bytes or behavior. The producer has to ensure that accepting any of the intended copies is valid.

\section{Reachability Is a Different Decision}

Levine's historical AIX example uses separate procedure sections and infers references from relocations \cite[chapter~11]{levine2000linkers}. A useful model for GNU ELF section collection is marking from roots rather than repeatedly deleting nodes with no incoming references. A dead cycle may never have zero incoming references, but no member of it is reachable. In a simplified model, mark the section of the entry symbol, any explicitly retained sections, then the targets of relocations in marked sections. Treat a retained group as a unit. Real linkers have further rules for exports, constructor arrays, unwind metadata, and similar special inputs; for example, a shared object's visible exports can be roots with GNU \texttt{ld}. In an executable, an ordinary global name is not automatically an export or a root. Hiding a definition does not prevent a live relocation from keeping it.

