\section{Keep a Registration Record, Not an Unreachable Cycle}

Assemble the blocks separately on x86-64 ELF with GNU \texttt{as}. Assemble the first block twice, as \texttt{oak.o} and \texttt{elm.o}, substituting \texttt{elm} for the string literal in the second copy. The differing strings deliberately expose the winner; they are not a model for equivalent C++ definitions.

\begin{verbatim}
# format.s: the function and private caption must travel together.
.section .text.format,"axG",@progbits,format,comdat
.globl format
format: leaq .Lcaption(%rip), %rax
        ret
.section .rodata.format,"aG",@progbits,format,comdat
.Lcaption: .asciz "oak"
\end{verbatim}

\begin{verbatim}
# roots.s: _start has no reference to the registration record.
.section .text.start,"ax",@progbits
.globl _start
_start: ret
.section .catalog,"a",@progbits
.quad format                    # One edge if the entry is live.
.section .text.loop_a,"ax",@progbits
loop_a: jmp loop_b
.section .text.loop_b,"ax",@progbits
loop_b: jmp loop_a               # Incoming edges alone prove nothing.
\end{verbatim}

Do not execute these fixtures. Inspect the input with \verb|readelf --section-groups oak.o|. Link with \verb|ld.bfd --gc-sections --print-gc-sections| followed by \verb|-o bare roots.o oak.o elm.o|. Without an explicit root for \texttt{.catalog}, neither that section nor \texttt{format} survives, and both loop symbols disappear. Now link with \verb|-T keep.ld| as well, using the following augmentation of GNU \texttt{ld}'s default script.

\begin{verbatim}
SECTIONS { .catalog : { KEEP(*(.catalog)) } }
INSERT AFTER .rodata;
\end{verbatim}

The catalog is now a root. Its pointer must equal the surviving \texttt{format} symbol's virtual address; the function must refer to its own caption. The dead loop still disappears. With the given order, GNU \verb|ld.bfd| keeps the \texttt{oak} copy; reverse the two objects and verify that the only caption in \verb|objdump -s -j .rodata| is now \texttt{elm}. Diagnose the result with a map (add \verb|-Map=kept.map|) and the GC log, not just a count of surviving names.

\Cref{fig:visual-289} separates duplicate selection from the root walk in the kept-catalog run.

