\section{Know Which Operation Removed the Bytes}

In the fixture the invariant is that every live catalog entry targets the selected group and that group keeps its matching caption, while no unrooted cycle survives. Add a second non-grouped strong definition of a fresh name to two ordinary inputs and expect a duplicate-definition diagnostic even with section GC enabled; GC is not a way to select an arbitrary definition.

To distinguish preservation from admission, move the catalog section and its pointer into a new object, give the pointer a global label \texttt{catalog\_entry}, and put that object alone in an archive. Keep the rest of \texttt{roots.o} on the link line. With the same script, the catalog is absent until you force \texttt{catalog\_entry} with \verb|-u catalog_entry| or link the new object explicitly. \verb|KEEP| protects matching sections of admitted objects; it does not request them from an archive.

Finally, identical code folding is a separate, optional operation. It can combine live sections with different names under a linker's equivalence policy. Raw bytes alone do not suffice: identical placeholders may relocate to different targets, and address equality can be observable even for otherwise equivalent functions. For an experiment on GC, leave folding disabled. Otherwise a missing contribution in the map could be evidence of folding rather than evidence that no root reached it.
